# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 001
sp("V04-D-001", "History and politics", "Guild inspection solved a real problem of trust for buyers, but the same powers could be turned to exclusion.", "Medium",
   ["Medieval craft guilds are usually remembered as cartels, bodies that fixed prices, limited entry and protected their members from competition.",
    "That reputation is not undeserved, but it leaves out a problem the guilds helped to solve.",
    "A buyer in a crowded town market had little way of judging whether a bolt of cloth was sound or a silver cup was of the promised purity.",
    "Seen this way, the guild's stamp was an early form of the brand, a signal that travelled further than any single maker's reputation could.",
    "The difficulty is that the powers needed to police quality were the same powers that could be used to keep newcomers out.",
    "An inspection regime that began by rejecting shoddy work could end by rejecting anyone who had not served an apprenticeship under an existing master.",
    "The history of the guilds is therefore less a story of good or bad institutions than of a useful tool that was easily turned to private ends."],
   "By inspecting workshops, stamping approved goods and punishing fraud, a guild lent its collective name to each object and so gave strangers a reason to trust it.",
   [1, 3, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence answers the buyer's problem of judging cloth and silver, so it must follow that sentence; and it introduces the stamping of approved goods, which the next sentence calls 'the guild's stamp' and interprets 'Seen this way'. Only gap (2) lies between the buyer's problem and the stamp.",
   {1: "At gap (1) no problem has yet been described for the inspection to solve, and the sentence would separate 'That reputation' from the cartel reputation it refers back to.",
    4: "At gap (3) the paragraph has already spoken of 'the guild's stamp' and interpreted it 'Seen this way', although nothing before it has mentioned any stamping; the explanation would arrive one sentence too late.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has turned to how quality control became exclusion, and the 'therefore' of the closing sentence must follow that example directly; returning to the benefits of inspection here breaks the conclusion."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), because it seems to begin a new point about the powers of inspection that the next sentence discusses.",
   "V04D-medieval-craft-guilds", "A backward link to the buyer's problem and a forward link to 'the guild's stamp' must both be tracked; gap (3) is tempting because the next sentence mentions policing quality.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 002
sp("V04-D-002", "History and politics", "Athenian ostracism removed a man who seemed too powerful without punishing him, and its freedom from proof made it open to abuse.", "Easy",
   ["Ostracism in classical Athens is often mentioned as a curiosity, but it was a carefully limited instrument.",
    "The man whose name appeared most often had to leave the city for ten years.",
    "He was not stripped of his property or his citizenship, and he could return with his standing intact once the period was over.",
    "The aim, in other words, was not to punish a crime but to remove a figure who seemed to be gathering too much influence before that influence could harden into tyranny.",
    "Because no charge had to be proved, however, the procedure was open to a quieter kind of abuse.",
    "A politician with organised supporters could hope to rid himself of a rival simply by persuading enough voters that the rival was dangerous.",
    "The very feature that made ostracism gentle, its freedom from the burden of proof, was also what made it easy to turn against inconvenient people."],
   "Each year the assembly first decided whether to hold an ostracism at all, and only if it voted to do so did citizens later scratch a name on a piece of broken pottery.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "The second sentence speaks of 'The man whose name appeared most often', which only makes sense once citizens have been described writing names. The missing sentence supplies that step, so it belongs at gap (1), right after the paragraph introduces ostracism as a limited instrument.",
   {2: "At gap (2) the sentence about 'The man whose name appeared most often' would already have come before any names were written, and 'He was not stripped' would then follow a sentence about the assembly rather than about the exiled man.",
    4: "At gap (3) the paragraph has already explained the aim of the procedure; describing how the vote was set up comes far too late, after its result has been discussed.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph is concluding about abuse of the procedure, so a description of its first step does not fit there."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), after the mention of exile, because it also describes the procedure.",
   "V04D-athenian-ostracism", "A plain backward reference ('The man whose name appeared most often') fixes the position; the other gaps fall after the procedure has been used.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

