sp("V04-D-003", "History and politics", "The trade around medieval shrines depended on belief, so it measures devotion rather than disproving it.", "Difficult",
   ["Historians of medieval pilgrimage once wrote almost entirely about devotion: the vows, the relics, the hope of healing or forgiveness.",
    "More recent work has stressed that the great shrines also sat at the centre of busy networks of buying and selling.",
    "Pilgrims needed food, beds, guides, ferries and safe passage, and towns along the main routes grew prosperous by supplying them.",
    "Much of it traded on devotion itself: badges, small flasks and other tokens were made and sold near the shrines in large numbers, and churches competed to acquire relics that would draw visitors.",
    "It is tempting to conclude from all this that faith was merely the occasion for trade, a pretext that the real business of the roads required.",
    "That conclusion reverses the dependence.",
    "The innkeepers and badge makers prospered only because travellers believed the journey was worth making, and when belief in a shrine's power faded, its trade usually faded with it.",
    "The economy of pilgrimage is better read as a measure of how seriously people took their devotions than as evidence that they did not."],
   "This trade, moreover, went well beyond what travellers strictly needed in order to complete the journey.",
   [1, 2, 3, 4],
   "The missing sentence adds ('moreover') a second point to the account of what pilgrims needed: the trade went beyond necessities. The sentence after it, 'Much of it traded on devotion itself', needs a singular 'it' that names this wider trade, and the badges and relics it lists are exactly what went beyond need. So the sentence sits between the list of necessities and the badges, at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) no trade has yet been mentioned, so 'This trade' has nothing to refer to; buying and selling are only introduced in the next sentence.",
    2: "At gap (2) the paragraph would say the trade went beyond necessities before it has described the necessities, and 'moreover' would add to a point that has not been made; the list of food and beds would then read as a step backwards.",
    4: "At gap (4) the badges and relics would already have been described, and their opening 'Much of it' would have had to refer back to food, beds and ferries, which were not traded 'on devotion itself'; the missing sentence would then also separate 'all this' from the evidence it sums up."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (4), where it seems to sum up the souvenir trade before the turn to the tempting conclusion.",
   "V04D-medieval-pilgrimage-economy", "Gap (4) looks like a natural summary point; the solver must see that 'Much of it' needs the missing sentence as its antecedent and that 'moreover' marks a new point rather than a summary.",
   exams=DX)

