sp("V04-D-004", "History and politics", "Volcanic cooling can cause harvest shortfalls, but whether they become famines depends on social arrangements.", "Medium",
   ["A very large volcanic eruption can throw enough fine particles into the upper atmosphere to dim sunlight across much of the globe for a year or more.",
    "Summers in the following seasons may turn cold and wet, frosts may arrive out of season, and harvests far from the volcano can fail.",
    "It is natural to read the famines that sometimes follow such events as simple consequences of the weather.",
    "The historical record suggests a more complicated picture.",
    "Regions that suffered similar losses of crops often fared very differently, depending on whether grain could be moved from places that had a surplus, whether stores had been kept and whether authorities acted quickly or hoarded.",
    "As a result, the same cold summer could mean hardship in one valley and mass death in the next."],
   "A volcano can cause a shortage, in short, but as those two valleys show, whether a shortage becomes a famine is decided largely by the arrangements people have made before it arrives.",
   [1, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence is a conclusion ('in short') that distinguishes a shortage from a famine and credits the difference to human arrangements. That conclusion depends on the evidence about grain transport, stores and authorities, and on the contrast between neighbouring valleys, to which 'those two valleys' refers, so it must close the paragraph at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the eruption itself has been described; no shortage or famine has been mentioned, so there is nothing for 'in short' to sum up.",
    3: "At gap (2) the sentence would state the complicated picture before the paragraph announces that 'The historical record suggests a more complicated picture', making that announcement redundant and putting the conclusion ahead of all its evidence.",
    5: "At gap (3) the conclusion would interrupt the argument, because 'As a result' in the last sentence draws its consequence from the regional differences just described and must follow them directly; 'those two valleys' would also point to valleys that have not yet been mentioned."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), treating the valley example as a vivid afterthought to the conclusion.",
   "V04D-volcanic-winters-harvests", "The solver must see that 'As a result' ties the valley example to the regional evidence and that 'those two valleys' needs that example, leaving the summary only one place.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 005
sp("V04-D-005", "Economics and business", "Putting a trusted brand on new products saves launch costs but risks blurring what the brand stands for.", "Easy",
   ["When a company has built a trusted brand, it is tempting to put that name on new kinds of products.",
    "The logic is easy to follow, since customers who already know the name may try the new product without the costly advertising a fresh brand would need.",
    "A maker of sturdy work boots might, for example, launch a line of jackets and bags under the same label.",
    "A brand is valuable because it stands for something definite in the buyer's mind, such as toughness, luxury or low price.",
    "If the name appears on too many unrelated goods, that definite meaning begins to blur.",
    "And if one of the new products disappoints, the damage may spread back to the original line that earned the trust in the first place.",
    "Firms that extend their brands successfully therefore tend to stay close to what the name already promises."],
   "Such extensions, however, carry a cost that does not show up in the launch budget.",
   [0, 2, 3, 6],
   "The missing sentence turns ('however') from the advantages of extending a brand to its hidden cost. It must follow the account of the benefits and the boots example of an extension, and it must come before the explanation of how the brand's meaning blurs. That position is gap (3).",
   {0: "At gap (1) the paragraph has not yet mentioned any extension, so 'Such extensions' has nothing to refer to and 'however' has nothing to contrast with.",
    2: "At gap (2) the turn to costs would come before the example, so 'for example' would then seem to illustrate the cost, although the boots maker's new line illustrates only the extension itself.",
    6: "At gap (4) the costs have already been set out, so 'however' would contrast a cost with other costs, and the closing 'therefore' must follow the account of the risks directly."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), right after the benefits, without noticing that the example that follows illustrates an extension rather than a cost.",
   "V04D-brand-line-extensions", "A contrast marker and a backward reference to the example fix one gap; the others are clearly off.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 006
sp("V04-D-006", "Economics and business", "Past spending is no reason to finish a project, except through the useful assets it has left behind.", "Medium",
   ["When a public project runs far over budget, the most common argument for finishing it is that too much has already been spent to stop.",
    "On that view, the only relevant question is whether the benefits still to come exceed the costs still to come.",
    "If they do not, every further rupee is wasted, however painful it is to admit that the earlier ones were wasted too.",
    "Yet the charge of sunk cost reasoning can be levelled too quickly.",
    "Part of what has been spent may have bought something real, such as cleared land, foundations or trained staff, that makes completion cheaper than starting afresh elsewhere.",
    "In that case the earlier spending matters, not because it has been spent but because of what it has left behind.",
    "The careful test, then, is to ask what the remaining work will cost and yield, counting the past only through its useful remains."],
   "Economists call this the sunk cost fallacy, because money that has already been spent cannot be recovered whichever way the decision goes.",
   [1, 3, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence names the argument just stated ('this') as a fallacy and gives the reason: past money cannot be recovered. The next sentence begins 'On that view', and the view it develops, that only future benefits and costs count, is the economists' view, not the argument for finishing. So the missing sentence must sit between them, at gap (1).",
   {3: "At gap (2) 'this' would be too far from the argument it names, and 'On that view' would already have referred back to the argument for finishing, which is the opposite of the view it states.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate 'Yet the charge of sunk cost reasoning can be levelled too quickly' from the example of cleared land and foundations that explains it.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already qualified the fallacy; naming it now would undo the qualification just before the closing 'then' draws its balanced test."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), as a label for the reasoning just explained, without seeing that 'On that view' needs the economists' view before it.",
   "V04D-sunk-costs-public-projects", "The solver must notice that 'On that view' cannot refer to the argument for finishing, since it states the opposite.")

