sp("V04-D-007", "Economics and business", "In common value auctions winning signals overestimation; the resulting losses come from bidders' reasoning, not the auction.", "Difficult",
   ["Imagine a jar of coins auctioned to a room of people, each of whom may study it but none of whom may count what is inside.",
    "Their guesses scatter around the true sum, some above it and some below, and their average may be fairly close to it.",
    "Yet the person who wins the jar is the one who offered the most, and that is usually the one who guessed highest.",
    "Victory, put differently, carries unwelcome information about the prize.",
    "A prudent bidder accordingly offers less than her own guess, asking what the jar would be worth on the assumption that her offer turned out to be the highest in the room.",
    "The correction is not intuitive, and those who neglect it may find that they consistently pay too much for exactly the things they most wanted to win."],
   "Such losses, however, are a fault in the bidders' reasoning rather than in the auction, which punishes only those who forget what their victory tells them.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence refers to 'Such losses', so it needs a prior mention of losing money; the only one is the last sentence, where those who neglect the correction 'consistently pay too much'. Its 'however' then qualifies that warning by placing the fault in the bidders rather than the auction. So it belongs at the end, gap (4).",
   {2: "At gap (1) only the guesses have been described; no one has won or lost anything, so 'Such losses' has no referent.",
    3: "At gap (2) the idea that the winner guessed highest might suggest a loss, but 'Victory, put differently' must restate the previous sentence directly, and the sentence would also pass judgment on bidders before the paragraph has said what a prudent bidder does.",
    5: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate 'The correction is not intuitive' from the correction it refers to, and the only thing mentioned just before it is prudent bidding, which produces no losses."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), reading the highest guesser as already having suffered a loss.",
   "V04D-auction-winners-curse", "Gap (2) is tempting because overestimation implies a loss; the solver must hold out for an explicit antecedent and respect 'put differently'.",
   exams=DX)

