sp("V04-D-008", "Economics and business", "Factory minimum orders reflect fixed set-up costs rather than disdain, yet they still burden small newcomers.", "Medium",
   ["Anyone who tries to start a small clothing label soon meets the minimum order quantity, the smallest batch a factory will agree to produce.",
    "To the beginner, these minimums can look like a way for large suppliers to brush off small customers.",
    "Cutting patterns, adjusting machines and dyeing fabric all take time and material before the first saleable piece appears.",
    "Spread over a large batch, that preparation adds little to the price of each item; spread over a small one, it can double it.",
    "The minimum is therefore less a snub than the point below which an order stops being worth the factory's trouble.",
    "Its effect on newcomers is real all the same, since it forces a founder to risk a large sum on stock before knowing whether anyone will buy.",
    "This is one reason why intermediaries who pool the orders of many small labels have found a place between the factories and the beginners."],
   "In fact they mostly reflect the cost of setting up a production run, which is much the same whether the factory then makes fifty garments or five thousand.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence corrects ('In fact') the beginner's impression that minimums are a brush-off, so it must follow that impression; and it introduces the cost of setting up a run, which the next sentence breaks into cutting, adjusting and dyeing. That places it at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the beginner's impression has not yet been stated, so 'In fact' corrects nothing, and the impression would then follow the explanation that refutes it.",
    4: "At gap (3) the set-up costs have already been described and their effect on price explained, so the sentence would repeat them, and 'In fact' would contradict the preparation sentence rather than the brush-off view.",
    5: "At gap (4) the verdict 'less a snub' has been reached; the sentence would separate it from 'Its effect on newcomers is real all the same', the concession that answers it."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), as a summary of the set-up costs just before the verdict.",
   "V04D-minimum-order-quantities", "A contrast marker and a forward link to the list of set-up tasks fix the gap; gap (3) is tempting as a summary.")

