sp("V04-D-009", "Economics and business", "Farmers' slowness to adopt better implements often reflected sound judgment of risk rather than habit.", "Difficult",
   ["Histories of farming are full of implements that were plainly better than the methods they replaced and yet spread with puzzling slowness.",
    "A drill that places seed in neat rows at an even depth, for instance, wastes less seed than scattering it by hand and makes weeding far easier.",
    "Yet a closer look at the conditions under which such tools were offered makes that suspicion look more like judgment.",
    "A drill earned its price on large, level fields well cleared of stones, and many farmers worked scattered strips of uneven ground.",
    "It also needed draught animals, repairs and some skill in setting it up, none of which came free.",
    "For a family whose margin between a fair year and a hungry one was narrow, an untested machine was a gamble whose losses would fall on them alone.",
    "What looks in hindsight like stubbornness was often a sound reading of risks that the promoters of the new tool did not have to bear."],
   "The customary explanation is that farmers were bound by habit and suspicious of anything their fathers had not used.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "The sentence beginning 'Yet a closer look' speaks of 'that suspicion', and nothing before it mentions any suspicion; the missing sentence supplies it ('suspicious of anything their fathers had not used'). The missing sentence also offers the explanation of the slow spread that the rest of the paragraph overturns, so it must come right after the drill example and before 'Yet', at gap (1).",
   {3: "At gap (2) the sentence would come after the sentence that 'makes that suspicion look more like judgment', so the rebuttal would precede the view it rebuts and 'that suspicion' would refer to nothing.",
    5: "At gap (3) the sentence would interrupt the list of what a drill required and what it risked, and it would introduce the habit explanation after most of the evidence against it has been given.",
    6: "At gap (4) the sentence seems to set up the closing contrast with 'stubbornness', but 'that suspicion' earlier in the paragraph would still have no antecedent, and the customary view would appear only after it had already been answered."},
   "Choosing gap (4), because the habit explanation looks like a natural foil for the closing word 'stubbornness'.",
   "V04D-seed-drill-adoption", "The closing 'stubbornness' pulls strongly towards gap (4); only by tracing 'that suspicion' back does the solver find the single gap that works.",
   exams=DX)

