sp("V04-D-012", "Science and environment", "The tidy story of returning wolves restoring a landscape outruns the evidence, though predators do matter.", "Difficult",
   ["The return of wolves to a large park in the American West is often told as a parable about the hidden connections of nature.",
    "In the usual telling, the wolves reduced and unsettled the elk, the elk stopped browsing young willows and aspens along the streams, and the recovering trees steadied the banks and brought back birds and beavers.",
    "The difficulty is that plausibility is not proof, and the park changed in many ways at once during the same years.",
    "Drought, hunting outside the park's boundaries and other predators all affected the elk, and along some streams the loss of beavers had altered the channels so much that willows could not easily return whatever the elk did.",
    "Researchers who have studied the same valleys disagree about how much of the change belongs to the wolves.",
    "None of this means that predators are unimportant, only that a single dramatic cause is easier to remember than a tangle of modest ones.",
    "A parable earns its place when it helps us notice connections; it misleads when it persuades us that the connections have been measured."],
   "Each link in that chain is plausible, and some have support from careful field studies.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "The phrase 'that chain' must be the sequence of wolves, elk, willows and banks set out in the usual telling. The missing sentence concedes that the links are plausible, and the next sentence answers it directly: 'The difficulty is that plausibility is not proof'. So it belongs right after the usual telling, at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the chain has not yet been described, so 'that chain' has no referent.",
    4: "At gap (3) the nearest thing to a list is the set of rival causes, drought, hunting and other predators, which act side by side rather than as links in a chain; and a concession placed here would come after 'The difficulty is that plausibility is not proof', which needs a claim of plausibility before it.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence would come after the disagreement among researchers, and 'None of this means that predators are unimportant' would then follow a sentence that has just affirmed the wolves' effects, making its defensive 'None of this' pointless."},
   "Choosing gap (3), treating the rival causes as 'that chain' and reading the sentence as a concession before the disagreement among researchers.",
   "V04D-wolves-trophic-cascade", "The concession could seem to fit at gap (3) or gap (4); the solver must see that only the usual telling forms a 'chain' and that the concession must come before 'The difficulty'.",
   exams=DX)

