sp("V04-D-013", "Science and environment", "Earthworms that help gardens damage northern forests that developed without them.", "Medium",
   ["Gardeners tend to regard earthworms as unmixed blessings, creatures that loosen the soil and turn dead leaves into rich earth.",
    "The glaciers of the last ice age appear to have wiped out any native worms there, and the forests that grew back after the ice retreated developed without them.",
    "Their floors came to be covered with a thick, slowly rotting layer of fallen leaves, in which tree seedlings, wildflowers and many small animals found shelter and food.",
    "Worms brought in with soil, potted plants and fishing bait can consume that layer within a few years.",
    "Where they have done so, the ground is left bare, and the plants and animals that depended on the leaf layer decline.",
    "The trees may be affected in time as well, since fewer seedlings survive to replace the old canopy.",
    "The creature that the gardener prizes for building soil is, in these forests, a force that takes apart something older."],
   "In many northern forests of North America, however, earthworms are recent arrivals.",
   [1, 3, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence turns ('however') from the gardener's view of worms to a region where they are newcomers. The next sentence needs it: 'there' must name a place, and 'the forests that grew back' must be the northern forests just mentioned. So it belongs at gap (1).",
   {3: "At gap (2) the sentence about glaciers would already have used 'there' with no place named, and 'however' would contrast the newcomers with the thick leaf layer, which is not a contrast at all.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate 'Where they have done so' from the worms consuming the leaf layer that the phrase refers to.",
    6: "At gap (4) the damage has been fully described; saying only now that worms are recent arrivals comes far too late, after the paragraph has depended on that fact throughout."},
   "Choosing gap (2), because 'recent arrivals' seems to lead naturally into 'Worms brought in with soil'.",
   "V04D-earthworm-invasions-forests", "Gap (2) reads smoothly with the next sentence; the solver must notice that 'there' earlier in the paragraph needs the place named in the missing sentence.")

