sp("V04-D-014", "Technology", "Following satellite directions eases travel but leaves people without a mental map of where they have been.", "Easy",
   ["Satellite navigation has made it hard to get lost in an unfamiliar city.",
    "A voice announces each turn shortly before it arrives, and the driver need only follow.",
    "People who navigate by spoken instructions tend to form a poorer picture of the area they have passed through than people who have worked out the route for themselves.",
    "They may reach the destination without being able to say in which direction it lay or how it related to places they already knew.",
    "For most journeys this hardly matters.",
    "It matters a good deal when the signal fails, the battery dies or the device sends the driver down a road that has been closed.",
    "A traveller who glances at the whole map before setting out, and checks it against the streets now and then, can keep most of the convenience while holding on to some of the old skill."],
   "Following, however, is not the same as learning the way.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence picks up the word 'follow' from the description of the voice announcing turns, and its 'however' turns from that convenience to its cost. The next sentence then explains the cost: people who navigate by instructions form a poorer picture of the area. So it belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) following has not yet been mentioned, since the voice and the driver who 'need only follow' come in the next sentence.",
    4: "At gap (3) the cost has already been explained in two sentences, so the sentence would repeat the point and break the link between 'They may reach the destination' and 'For most journeys this hardly matters'.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph is offering its practical advice; returning to the difference between following and learning there adds nothing and 'however' would contrast with nothing."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (1), right after the claim that it is hard to get lost.",
   "V04D-satellite-navigation-wayfinding", "A repeated word and a contrast marker fix the position; the other gaps are clearly off.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 015
sp("V04-D-015", "Technology", "Technical debt, like financial debt, is not bad in itself; the danger is shortcuts that nobody records.", "Medium",
   ["Programmers speak of technical debt when they take a shortcut that makes software work now but harder to change later.",
    "The metaphor is apt in more ways than one.",
    "Like a loan, a shortcut gives a team something immediately, perhaps a release in time for an important customer, in exchange for costs that come later.",
    "Like a loan, too, it charges interest, since every new feature built on top of the hurried code takes longer to write and is more likely to break.",
    "The metaphor also implies, rightly, that debt is not always a mistake.",
    "A young company that borrows time to reach the market before its money runs out may be choosing wisely.",
    "Teams that record their shortcuts and set aside time to repay them can borrow freely; teams that forget them eventually find that most of their effort goes on interest."],
   "The danger lies elsewhere, in borrowing that nobody writes down.",
   [1, 3, 4, 6],
   "The word 'elsewhere' needs a preceding statement of where the danger does not lie, and that is the claim that debt 'is not always a mistake', illustrated by the young company. The missing sentence then names the real danger, unrecorded borrowing, which the last sentence develops by contrasting teams that record their shortcuts with teams that forget them. So it belongs at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) nothing has yet been said about where danger does or does not lie, and the sentence would separate 'The metaphor is apt' from the metaphor it refers to.",
    3: "At gap (2) the sentence would split the two parallel sentences beginning 'Like a loan', whose 'too' ties the second directly to the first.",
    4: "At gap (3) the paragraph has just described interest as a real cost; saying that the danger lies 'elsewhere' would contradict that, because the claim that borrowing can be wise has not yet been made."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), reading 'elsewhere' as a contrast with the interest described just before it.",
   "V04D-technical-debt-software", "The solver must find what 'elsewhere' contrasts with; gap (3) is tempting because a cost has just been described.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 016
sp("V04-D-016", "Technology", "The refrigerator changed the rhythm of shopping and eating, and also made waste easier.", "Medium",
   ["The household refrigerator is usually described as a device for keeping food from spoiling, and so it is.",
    "Its larger effect, though, was on the rhythm of domestic life.",
    "Where food could be kept cold for days, the daily trip to market could become a weekly one, and shopping could be done in much bigger loads.",
    "Leftovers became a normal part of planning meals rather than something to be eaten at once or thrown away.",
    "The same convenience had a less welcome side, because food that can be stored can also be forgotten.",
    "A good deal of household waste consists of food bought in the confidence that the refrigerator would keep it, and then left at the back until it could not.",
    "The machine that freed people from the daily market also loosened the close watch on supplies that the daily market had enforced."],
   "Shoppers buying in such loads in turn favoured large stores with room to park over the small grocer on the corner.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The phrase 'such loads' must refer to a mention of large quantities of shopping, and the only one is the move from daily trips to weekly shopping 'in much bigger loads'. Shoppers carrying large loads in turn need big stores with parking, so the sentence follows the shopping sentence, at gap (3), before the paragraph returns to leftovers.",
   {1: "At gap (1) the only thing said is that refrigerators keep food from spoiling; no shopping, let alone shopping in large loads, has been mentioned, so 'such loads' has no referent.",
    2: "At gap (2) the 'larger effect' on domestic rhythm has been announced but not yet described; the bigger loads that 'such loads' refers to come only in the next sentence.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has turned to forgotten food and waste; a sentence about stores would break the link between 'food that can be stored can also be forgotten' and the waste that illustrates it, and 'such loads' would reach back three sentences."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), as an example of the 'larger effect' on domestic life, before the bigger loads have been mentioned.",
   "V04D-domestic-refrigeration-diet", "Gap (2) tempts because the sentence seems to illustrate the 'larger effect'; the solver must find the antecedent of 'such loads' and accept the return to domestic life afterwards.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 017
sp("V04-D-017", "Technology", "Energy per kilogram decides some battery choices, but for most uses other qualities matter as much or more.", "Difficult",
   ["Discussions of batteries tend to fix on a single number, the amount of energy that can be stored in each kilogram.",
    "For a phone or an electric aircraft, where every gram must be carried, that number is rightly decisive.",
    "A battery that stores a great deal of energy but loses much of its capacity after a few hundred charges, for example, is a poor choice for a grid store that will be cycled every day for twenty years.",
    "One that can be recharged quickly may matter more to a bus fleet than one that holds a little more.",
    "And the chemistries that pack in the most energy are often the hardest to keep from overheating, which counts for a great deal in a building where people sleep.",
    "Gains in energy per kilogram are therefore real gains, but they are gains along one axis of a problem that has several."],
   "For most other purposes, however, energy per kilogram is only one virtue among several, and not always the one that matters most.",
   [2, 3, 4, 5],
   "The grid example begins with 'for example', so it must illustrate a general claim stated just before it; the claim that density is 'rightly decisive' for phones is not what it illustrates. The missing sentence supplies that claim: for other purposes density is only one virtue among several. It also turns ('however') from phones and aircraft to 'other purposes'. So it belongs at gap (1).",
   {3: "At gap (2) the grid example would already have appeared with 'for example' and no general claim to illustrate, and the missing sentence would then interrupt the run of examples.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would split the bus example from the overheating example, whose opening 'And' adds it to the same run, and the earlier 'for example' would still lack its claim.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence looks like a summary before the conclusion, but 'however' would have nothing to contrast with, since the overheating example already concerns a drawback of high density, and the grid example's 'for example' would still have nothing to illustrate."},
   "Choosing gap (4), because the missing sentence resembles the conclusion's 'one axis of a problem that has several' and seems to introduce it.",
   "V04D-battery-energy-density", "The missing sentence reads like a summary and pulls towards gap (4); only the 'for example' in the grid sentence shows that the general claim must come first.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 018
sp("V04-D-018", "Arts and literature", "Jazz improvisation is freedom built on mastered material, like fluent speech.", "Easy",
   ["To a listener hearing it for the first time, a jazz solo can sound like pure invention, as if the player were making everything up on the spot.",
    "In one sense that is true, since no two performances of a solo are ever quite the same.",
    "But the invention rests on years of preparation.",
    "Players learn the chords of hundreds of tunes, practise phrases until their fingers find them without thought, and study the recordings of those who came before them.",
    "When they improvise, they are combining and bending this stored material in response to the moment, much as a fluent speaker builds new sentences out of familiar words.",
    "The freedom the listener hears is real, but it is the freedom of someone who has mastered a language, not of someone who has escaped from one."],
   "A speaker with no words to draw on would not be more spontaneous, only silent.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence extends the comparison with 'a fluent speaker' who builds sentences from familiar words, so it must come right after that comparison. It also prepares the closing point that real freedom belongs to someone who has mastered a language. So it belongs at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) no speaker or language has been mentioned, so the sentence would have nothing to attach to, and it would separate 'In one sense that is true' from the claim it agrees with.",
    2: "At gap (2) the paragraph has only granted that solos differ each time; the comparison with speech has not yet been made.",
    3: "At gap (3) the paragraph has announced the years of preparation but not yet described them or compared them with speech, so a remark about a speaker without words comes too early."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), as a comment on preparation, before the speech comparison has been made.",
   "V04D-jazz-improvisation-rules", "The missing sentence extends an analogy made in a single earlier sentence; the other gaps come before the analogy.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 019
sp("V04-D-019", "Arts and literature", "Silent film acting was a craft suited to an image-only medium, not a failure to be natural.", "Medium",
   ["Modern audiences watching silent films often laugh at the acting, with its rolling eyes, clutched hearts and arms flung toward the sky.",
    "The laughter assumes that the performers simply did not know how to behave naturally in front of a camera.",
    "Apart from the occasional title card, every feeling and every turn of the plot had to be carried by the face and body, often for audiences sitting far back in large halls.",
    "Gestures that look overblown on a modern screen were a way of making meaning legible when the image was almost the only channel available.",
    "Nor was the style fixed, for by the later years of the silent era many actors had developed a quieter manner, helped by closer camera work.",
    "To judge the earlier style by the standards of the sound film is a little like faulting a stage actor for speaking loudly enough to reach the back row."],
   "A better explanation is that they were solving a different problem from the one that actors face today.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "'A better explanation' needs an explanation to improve on, and that is the assumption behind the laughter, that the performers did not know how to be natural. The 'different problem' it announces is then described in the next sentence: everything had to be carried by face and body. So the sentence belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) no explanation has yet been offered, so 'A better explanation' has nothing to be better than, and 'they' would have to mean the modern audiences.",
    4: "At gap (3) the different problem has already been described and the gestures explained as a solution, so announcing that problem now would come after the point has been made.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has moved on to the later, quieter style, and the closing comparison with a stage actor follows from the whole account; announcing a better explanation there is far too late."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), as a summary of the account of gestures, rather than as the announcement that sets it up.",
   "V04D-silent-film-acting", "A backward reference to the rejected assumption and a forward set-up for the 'different problem' fix the gap; gap (3) tempts as a summary.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 020
sp("V04-D-020", "Arts and literature", "Publication in parts shaped Victorian fiction, and its supposed compromises are much of what readers enjoy.", "Difficult",
   ["A good deal of Victorian fiction was sold first in parts, a few chapters at a time, so that a single story might hold its public for well over a year.",
    "That mode of publication shaped the books in ways that survive in the bound editions we read today.",
    "Every part had to finish on something that would carry its buyer through the month until the next, which is why so many chapters break off at a discovery, a threat or a question left hanging.",
    "That month's wait also rewarded crowded casts and sharply drawn peculiarities, since a reader had to recognise figures last encountered several weeks earlier.",
    "Some critics have regarded these traits as compromises with the market, the sort of thing a writer under no such pressure would have avoided.",
    "The verdict is too hasty.",
    "The crowding, the suspense and the loose, accommodating plots are not flaws laid over a purer design; they account for much of the pleasure these books still give, and they arose from the very circumstances being deplored."],
   "Since the closing chapters did not yet exist when the opening ones went on sale, an author could also take account of readers' reactions, giving more room to a favourite character or altering a storyline that had displeased them.",
   [2, 3, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence adds ('also') a further effect of publication in parts to a list already begun, so it cannot open the list. The two effects already listed are tied together, because 'That month's wait' refers back to 'the month until the next'. So the missing sentence must follow both and come before the critics' view of 'these traits', at gap (3). It also accounts for the 'loose, accommodating plots' in the last sentence.",
   {2: "At gap (1) no particular effect of the form has yet been described, so 'also' would add to nothing; the list of effects begins only with the chapter endings in the next sentence.",
    3: "At gap (2) the sentence would separate 'That month's wait' from 'the month until the next' to which it refers, leaving the sentence about crowded casts with no antecedent.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence would come between the critics' view and 'The verdict is too hasty', which must answer that view directly."},
   "Choosing gap (2), treating the effects as a list whose order does not matter, without noticing that 'That month's wait' binds the second effect to the first.",
   "V04D-serialised-novels-cliffhangers", "The sentence is one item in a list, and list items usually seem movable; the solver must use 'also' and the link 'That month's wait' to fix its place.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 021
sp("V04-D-021", "Arts and literature", "Modernists rejected ornament as dishonest, but some of it had practical functions that plain surfaces lost.", "Medium",
   ["Some early modernist architects attacked ornament as a waste of labour and a disguise, a layer of borrowed decoration that hid what a building really was.",
    "Such surfaces were meant to be honest, and in the best buildings of the movement they are.",
    "Yet ornament had often done more than decorate.",
    "Mouldings threw shadows that showed the eye how large a wall was, carved bands marked the floors from the street, and projecting sills and cornices kept rain off the face of the building.",
    "When these were stripped away, some of their work went undone, and a good many plain facades of the last century have weathered into streaked and stained surfaces that look neither honest nor new.",
    "The lesson is not that the modernists were wrong to want clarity, but that they mistook some of ornament's practical functions for mere fashion."],
   "Their alternative was the plain surface, in which structure and material were left to speak for themselves.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "'Their alternative' needs the architects who attacked ornament, so it follows the first sentence; and the next sentence's 'Such surfaces' needs the plain surface that the missing sentence introduces. So it sits between the first two sentences, at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) the sentence 'Such surfaces were meant to be honest' would already have come with no surfaces named, and 'Their' would refer to surfaces rather than to the architects.",
    3: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate 'Yet ornament had often done more than decorate' from the list of mouldings, bands and cornices that explains it.",
    5: "At gap (4) 'Their' would most naturally refer to the weathered facades just mentioned, and introducing the modernists' alternative after its failures have been described reverses the argument."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), because 'Their alternative' seems to answer the praise of honest surfaces.",
   "V04D-architectural-ornament-modernism", "A backward reference ('Their') and a forward one ('Such surfaces') fix the gap; the solver must notice that the second sentence cannot stand without it.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 022
sp("V04-D-022", "Language and media", "Emoji restore to brief written messages the tone that speech carries naturally.", "Easy",
   ["In speech, the same words can sound friendly or cutting depending on tone of voice, facial expression and timing.",
    "A short written message strips all of these away.",
    "A reply such as 'Fine.' may be meant as simple agreement and read as cold irritation.",
    "A smiling face added to the same word tells the reader how it is meant to be taken, much as a smile would in conversation.",
    "Critics who see emoji as a sign that writing is in decline overlook the fact that quick messages between friends were never meant to be formal prose.",
    "Emoji are better understood as a way of putting back into text something that speech has always carried without effort."],
   "It is this kind of misreading that emoji are largely used to prevent.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The phrase 'this kind of misreading' needs an example of a message being misread, and the only one is the reply 'Fine.' meant as agreement but read as irritation. The sentence then introduces emoji, which the next sentence illustrates with a smiling face. So it belongs at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only described tone in speech; no misreading has been mentioned.",
    2: "At gap (2) the paragraph has said that writing strips tone away, but no actual misreading has yet been described, so 'this kind of misreading' comes one sentence too early.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence would follow the remark about critics, so 'this kind of misreading' would seem to refer to the critics' view, and it would separate the critics' remark from 'Emoji are better understood', which answers it."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (2), treating the loss of tone as if it were already a misreading.",
   "V04D-emoji-tone-texting", "A backward reference to a specific example fixes the gap; gap (2) is the only close alternative and fails because no misreading has been given yet.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 023
sp("V04-D-023", "Language and media", "Contradictory proverbs are tools for different situations, not conflicting laws.", "Medium",
   ["Collections of proverbs are easily mocked, because so many of them contradict one another.",
    "'Look before you leap' sits beside 'He who hesitates is lost', and 'Many hands make light work' beside 'Too many cooks spoil the broth'.",
    "If proverbs were meant as general laws, such pairs would show that popular wisdom cannot make up its mind.",
    "But they are better understood as tools than as laws.",
    "In the same way, each proverb names a pattern that really does occur and offers it to someone who must decide whether the present case is an instance of it.",
    "Wisdom, on this view, lies less in knowing the proverbs than in knowing which of them the moment in front of us calls for."],
   "A carpenter who owns both a saw and a glue pot is not confused about whether wood should be cut or joined; he uses each when the job requires it.",
   [1, 2, 3, 4],
   "The carpenter is an example of the claim that proverbs are 'tools' rather than laws, so it must follow that claim. The next sentence begins 'In the same way', which needs a comparison just before it, and applies the carpenter's practice to proverbs. So the sentence belongs at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has not yet given any contradictory proverbs or mentioned tools, so the carpenter has nothing to illustrate.",
    2: "At gap (2) the sentence would separate the pairs of proverbs from 'such pairs', which refers back to them.",
    3: "At gap (3) the carpenter would appear before tools have been mentioned, and 'But they are better understood as tools' would then seem to contradict the carpenter rather than introduce him."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), as an illustration of the idea that proverbs are not laws, before the word 'tools' has been introduced.",
   "V04D-contradictory-proverbs", "Example after claim, locked by 'In the same way'; gap (3) tempts because the carpenter seems to answer the charge of contradiction.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 024
sp("V04-D-024", "Language and media", "Puns are the visible difficulty of translating jokes; jokes that rely on shared assumptions fail more quietly.", "Difficult",
   ["It is often said that jokes are the hardest thing to translate, and puns are the usual example.",
    "A pun depends on two meanings sharing one sound, and the chance that another language offers the same coincidence is small.",
    "Many jokes contain no wordplay at all and still fail to travel, because they lean on what the audience is assumed to know already: a stereotype about a neighbouring town, the habits of a profession, an advertisement everyone has seen.",
    "Translated word for word, such a joke arrives intact and is not funny, and nothing on the page shows why, since the assumption it needed has been left behind.",
    "The translator then faces a choice between keeping the words and keeping the laugh.",
    "Keeping the laugh usually means replacing the reference with a local one, so that what the new audience enjoys is, strictly speaking, a different joke built on the same frame."],
   "Yet when a pun fails in this way, the failure is at least plain to see, and in that sense puns are the easy case.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The phrase 'fails in this way' needs a description of how a pun fails, which the second sentence gives: another language rarely offers the same coincidence of sound. The missing sentence then calls puns 'the easy case', which sets up the harder case of jokes with 'no wordplay at all' whose failure is not visible. So it belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has said only that puns are the usual example; it has not described how a pun fails, so 'in this way' has no referent.",
    3: "At gap (3) the jokes just described contain no wordplay, so 'when a pun fails in this way' would not match them, and the sentence would separate 'such a joke' from the jokes it refers to.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence would interrupt the translator's choice, separating 'Keeping the laugh' from the choice between words and laugh that it takes up."},
   "Choosing gap (3), because the contrast between visible and hidden failure seems to fit right after the jokes that fail quietly.",
   "V04D-translating-jokes-humour", "Gap (3) is tempting because it sits at the contrast between the two kinds of joke; the solver must match 'in this way' to the account of how puns fail and respect 'such a joke'.",
   exams=DX)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 025
sp("V04-D-025", "Language and media", "Punctuation began as a guide to pauses and became a guide to grammar, and the mixture explains today's disputes.", "Easy",
   ["Many of the marks we now call punctuation began as aids to reading aloud.",
    "As silent reading became normal and printed books multiplied, the marks gradually took on a different job.",
    "They came to show the structure of sentences, separating clauses and setting off phrases, whether or not a speaker would pause there.",
    "The two purposes have never been fully separated, and modern usage carries traces of both.",
    "This is why writers still argue about commas, since a comma that marks a breath and a comma that marks a grammatical boundary do not always fall in the same place.",
    "Much of the confusion about punctuation comes from treating a mixed inheritance as if it were a single, consistent design."],
   "They told a reader where to pause and for how long, so that a text could be delivered clearly to listeners.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence describes the first job of the marks, guiding pauses for reading aloud, so it must follow the opening sentence about 'aids to reading aloud'. The next sentence speaks of 'a different job', which needs the first job to have been described. So it belongs at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) the paragraph has just announced 'a different job', so describing the old job here would break the link between that announcement and 'They came to show the structure of sentences'.",
    3: "At gap (3) both jobs would already have been named, and a return to the old one would come after the new one has been described and just before 'The two purposes', which sums them up.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph is drawing its conclusion about commas and confusion; describing the old job of the marks there comes far too late."},
   "Placing the sentence at gap (3), next to 'The two purposes', as if it were needed there to recall the first purpose.",
   "V04D-punctuation-history-reading-aloud", "A forward link to 'a different job' and a backward link to 'reading aloud' fix the gap; the other gaps are clearly off.",
   cognitive="Understanding")
